    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The first day, 1 July 1916, cost the British 57,470 casualties, 19,240 of them killed, the worst in the history of the British Army, most of them north of the Albert–Bapaume road; the French Sixth Army lost 1,590.</li><li>The plan changed as the war went on: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> drew off French divisions, so the offensive in which the French were to take the main part became a mainly British effort, and a hoped-for decisive victory was reduced to relieving Verdun and wearing down the German army.</li><li>Casualty counts are disputed: British 419,654 to 432,000, French about 200,000, and German anywhere from 400,000 to 680,000 depending on the historian and the method of counting.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Before the attack on 1 July the British artillery fired more than 1.5 million shells in the preliminary bombardment, more than in the first year of the war, and the guns could be heard on Hampstead Heath, 165 miles (266 km) away.</p></aside>
    </section>

